travelling in a bus through southern  afghanistan  these passengers were approaching a  checkpoint around midnight  when their bus drove over a mine their  journey now one of recovery in merwice  regional hospital  the latest casualties in an increasingly  insecure country  when our vehicle approached the  checkpoint the bomb exploded many people  were killed and  injured i saw people's hands and feet  had fallen to the ground  they were not themselves two more bomb  blasts followed one in par one that  killed two passengers  another targeted soldiers in western  pharaoh province killing several  in afghanistan this was a bloody weekend  on monday a hillside in western kabul  was scored with dozens of fresh graves  teenage girls were targeted as they left  school on saturday with three separate  bombs  it was the deadliest attack in more than  a year and targeted members of the  hazara ethnic group  a sheer minority according to the new  york times in the weeks starting april  30th  140 government forces and over 40  civilians were killed across  afghanistan the highest death toll in a  single week  since october the afghan government said  the taliban were responsible  the taliban blamed isil and afghan  intelligence services  these are not our goals killing innocent  people even these are against our goals  because our goal is protection of for  the common people  protection of places national projects  that we have announced the rise in  casualties has coincided with the final  pull-out of foreign troops  may 1 was the former deadline for the  withdrawal of american troops from  afghanistan  u.s president joe biden pushed that back  to september 11th  their nato allies will leave then also  eu foreign ministers met on monday the  withdrawal from afghanistan at the  forefront of discussions  after the terrible attacks in the recent  days it's all the more important for the  eu to make very clear that afghanistan  and the afghan government can continue  to count on europe's support  we'll do everything we can to ensure the  ongoing peace negotiations reach a  conclusion  a key goal in u.s and nato negotiations  with afghan and taliban representatives  has always been a reduction in violence  and after an horrific weekend  some progress the taliban announced a  rare three-day ceasefire for the muslim  eid holidays  it will be only the third break in  fighting in nearly three years  a brief reprieve for those caught in the  middle charlotte ballas  al jazeera well may has been the  deadliest month in afghanistan this year  it coincides with the ongoing withdrawal  of u.s and nato troops  ending a 20-year presence in the country  the most recent attack on a girls school  on saturday in kabul killed more than 50  people most of them students  at least 160 pro-government forces and  over 65 civilians have been killed in  separate incidents  bringing the total death toll to over  220 people  in just the last 10 days the united  states under the trump administration  agreed to withdraw  all its troops by may 1st but washington  missed that deadline the pull-out began  this month  and is expected to end by september the  11th as per the new deadline set by  president biden  bahia is a lecturer of peace and  conflict mediation at cardinal  university he joins us now  live via skype from kabul good to have  you with us this time  yesterday i was talking to the taliban's  media spokesperson who was responding to  the government's assertion  uh that the taliban was responsible for  that blast  at the school on saturday it wasn't us  he said  how can you be so sure i asked him is  the taliban united  in its aims and objectives  uh first off escalation violence is a  very natural  part of peace processes because  different parties use it for leverage  again the taliban denying responsibility  serves them very well  because they tend to avoid international  scrutiny  while achieving military goals  the more important and difficult  question to ask is  if truly the taliban are not behind  these attacks then  there is a group here that has the  capacity and the sophistication to carry  such uh complicated attacks  however there is also a discord or the  better term to be used is tension  amongst the taliban leadership as well  the taliban leadership  is divided into moderates um that  include mullah brother and  malaya and on the other end they're  hardliners like  zada who heads the taliban movement  and this escalation and violence sort of  creates a polarity  amongst them which makes it very  difficult to reconcile visions at the  end of the day  the problem with this escalation in  violence of course  is that that the taliban is supposed to  be preparing to to  integrate its fighters into the system  how does it do that  uh when people are so  against it because of what it's doing to  ordinary people in afghanistan  again the second level of polarity  exists among the  foreign society as well iran people  don't even understand  what the utility of peace is anymore  with everything that is happening  um we need to also understand that there  is um  peace is not an event it's a process  right reconciliation has to start with  building mindsets towards peace uh  according to the  united states institute of peace  mohammed mansoor back the deceased  taliban leader back in 2015-16  had already started conducting workshops  for taliban fighters  to get them soften them up to the  prospects of peace  now with the escalation and violence  what i fear is  that if the taliban fighters are  smelling military victory  um they will not give into any  settlement because at the end of the day  if a total war and total victory can be  achieved  why settle why compromise that would be  something that's going in the heads of  the  fighters of the taliban that's something  that the taliban leadership  seriously need to address because these  fighters will not  change within a day will not join the  political system will not reintegrate  um there has to be a gradual reduction  in violence  for us to achieve that goal really good  to talk to you so many thanks indeed to  obey dollar by here there  in kabul
 

